1. git init

Purpose:
Initializes a new Git repository.
Creates a hidden .git directory.
Command: git init

2. git config

Purpose:
Configure Git username and email.
Used to identify who created the commits.

Commands:
git config --global user.name "Your Name"
git config --global user.email "your@email.com"
Verify: git config --list

3. git add

Purpose
Moves files from Working Directory to Staging Area.

Commands
git add filename
git add .
git add *.sh

4. git commit

Purpose
Saves staged changes permanently into Git history.

Command: git commit -m "Commit Message"
Example: git commit -m "Added Login Module"

5. git log

Purpose
Shows commit history.

Command: git log
Short format: git log --oneline

6. git checkout <commit-id>

Purpose:
View an old commit.
HEAD moves to that commit.
Repository enters Detached HEAD state.

Command: git checkout <commit-id>
Return to branch: git checkout master

7. HEAD

Purpose:
HEAD points to the current commit.
Normally it points to the latest commit of the current branch.
After checking out a commit ID, HEAD becomes detached.

8. git revert

Purpose
Undo a commit safely.
Creates a new commit.
Original commit is NOT deleted.

Command: git revert <commit-id>
Example:   A → B → C
After:  A → B → C → Revert C
Real Time: Used after code is pushed to GitHub.

9. git reset --hard

Purpose
Move the branch back to a previous commit.
Removes commits after the selected commit from the current branch.
Restores files to that commit's state.

Command: git reset --hard <commit-id>

Example

Before: A → B → C
After: A Use Mostly for local changes before sharing.

10. .gitignore

Purpose
Ignore files and folders that Git should not track.

Example
*.log
*.tmp
node_modules/

11. git branch

Purpose:List all local branches.

Command: git branch
Create branch: git branch <branchname>

12. git checkout

Purpose: Switch to another branch.

Command:  git checkout <branchname>

13. git checkout -b

Purpose: Create a new branch and switch to it.

Command: git checkout -b feature

14. Delete Branch

Delete merged branch #git branch -d branchname
Force delete #git branch -D branchname

15. git merge

Purpose: Merge one branch into the current branch.

Command: git merge branchname

Example

master
   \
    feature

After merge

master
   |
feature

16. Remote Repository

Purpose:
A Remote Repository is a central Git repository hosted on GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket, or Azure DevOps.
Used for collaboration, backup, and CI/CD integration.

Example:

Local Repository  ←→  GitHub Remote Repository
17. git remote add

Purpose:
Connect a local repository to a remote repository.

Command:

git remote add origin <repository-url>

Example:

git remote add origin https://github.com/reddy-sv/Devops.git
18. origin

Purpose:
origin is the default alias (short name) for the remote repository.
Instead of typing the full GitHub URL every time, Git uses the name origin.

Example:

origin
   │
   ▼
https://github.com/reddy-sv/Devops.git
19. git remote

Purpose:
Displays the configured remote repository names.

Command:

git remote

Example Output:

origin
20. git remote -v

Purpose:
Displays the remote repository name along with Fetch and Push URLs.

Command:

git remote -v

Example Output:

origin  https://github.com/reddy-sv/Devops.git (fetch)
origin  https://github.com/reddy-sv/Devops.git (push)
21. git push

Purpose:
Uploads local commits to the remote repository.

Command:

git push origin branchname

Example:

git push origin Main
22. git push -u

Purpose:
Pushes the branch and sets the upstream (tracking) branch.

Command:

git push -u origin feature

Next Time:

git push

(No need to specify the branch again.)

23. git clone

Purpose:
Copies an existing remote repository to your local machine.

Command:

git clone <repository-url>

Example:

git clone https://github.com/reddy-sv/Devops.git
24. git fetch

Purpose:
Downloads the latest changes from the remote repository without merging them into the current branch.

Command:

git fetch origin
25. git pull

Purpose:
Downloads the latest changes from the remote repository and merges them into the current branch.

Command:

git pull origin Main

Formula:

git pull = git fetch + git merge
26. Fetch vs Pull
git fetch	git pull
Downloads changes only	Downloads and merges changes
Safe to review changes	Updates current branch immediately
27. Pull Request (PR)

Purpose:
Request to merge changes from one branch into another branch.

Workflow:

Feature Branch
      │
      ▼
Pull Request
      │
      ▼
Main Branch
28. When do we create a Pull Request?

Real-Time Scenarios:

Bug Fix
New Feature
Enhancement
Terraform Changes
Jenkinsfile Changes
Kubernetes YAML Changes
Dockerfile Changes
Documentation Updates
Hotfixes
